---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const questions = [
  { question: 'Does ENSO-neutral mean normal weather everywhere?', answer: 'No. Neutral describes the tropical Pacific ENSO state, not a promise of average weather at every location. Other climate influences, long-term trends and individual weather systems still affect local conditions.' },
  { question: 'Is ENSO-neutral the same as equal chances on a CPC map?', answer: 'No. ENSO-neutral is one possible tropical Pacific state. Equal chances on a regional temperature or precipitation map means no favored category among above-, near- and below-normal outcomes. The two products answer different questions.' },
  { question: 'Does a forecast return to neutral mean La Niña comes next?', answer: 'No. Neutral can occur between events, but ENSO does not follow a fixed alternation. Use the published probabilities for all three phases and their target seasons; a neutral forecast cannot establish the following winter’s phase.' },
];
---
<Layout title="What Is ENSO-Neutral? Weather, Forecast Odds & Equal Chances" active="forecast" description="ENSO-neutral is neither El Niño nor La Niña. Learn why it does not mean normal local weather, how to read neutral probabilities and why equal chances is different.">
  <div class="page-shell subpage lifecycle-page">
    <div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">THE THIRD ENSO STATE · REVIEWED NOVEMBER 12, 2026</span><h1>What does<br /><span>ENSO-neutral mean?</span></h1><p>Neither El Niño nor La Niña. It describes the tropical Pacific, not a guarantee of ordinary weather where you live.</p></div></div>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Neutral is more than one temperature reading.</h2><p class="body-copy">The National Weather Service defines neutral as neither El Niño nor La Niña. Tropical Pacific sea-surface temperatures are often close to average, but the NWS also notes cases where the ocean appears to resemble one phase while the atmosphere does not respond accordingly. ENSO is a coupled ocean-atmosphere system.</p><p class="body-copy">A weekly Niño 3.4 value crossing a threshold cannot by itself confirm a transition to neutral. Weekly observations, three-month indices and CPC's assessment describe different things. Check the <a href="/">current official assessment</a> and its issue date; this guide explains the state without declaring that it is currently present.</p></section>
    <figure class="guide-figure"><a href="/images/guides/enso-winter-circulation-noaa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/guides/enso-winter-circulation-noaa.jpg" alt="NOAA schematic comparing typical late-fall to early-spring jet-stream patterns during La Niña and El Niño" width="1600" height="766" /></a><figcaption>NOAA schematic reproduced by NWS: typical late-fall through early-spring circulation during moderate to strong La Niña and El Niño. This is a comparison of the two event phases, not a neutral-phase map or a forecast for winter 2026–27. <a href="https://www.weather.gov/mhx/ensowhat">Source and explanation</a>.</figcaption></figure>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Why can neutral still bring unusual weather?</h2><p class="body-copy">ENSO changes large-scale circulation and the odds of some seasonal patterns; it is one influence on climate, not the only one. Its neutral state does not switch off atmospheric variability, individual storms or long-term warming. It also does not establish equal chances for every region.</p><p class="body-copy">A regional seasonal outlook can still favor warmth, cold, wetness or dryness during a neutral period. Read that product directly. For a particular journey or storm, the <a href="https://www.weather.gov/">local weather forecast and warnings</a> remain the relevant source in every ENSO phase.</p></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Three meanings that are easy to confuse</h2><div class="probability-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="ENSO neutral versus regional outlook categories" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table"><caption>Different products · different questions</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Term</th><th scope="col">What it describes</th><th scope="col">What it does not promise</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">ENSO-neutral</th><td>The tropical Pacific is assessed as neither event phase</td><td>Near-normal weather at every location</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Near-normal category</th><td>The middle category for a region's seasonal temperature or precipitation</td><td>Ordinary weather on every day</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Equal chances (EC)</th><td>No favored regional category; about one-third chance for each</td><td>A forecast specifically favoring near-normal conditions</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>How to read a neutral probability</h2><p class="body-copy">In the <a href="/forecast/">CPC phase forecast</a>, each row gives El Niño, neutral and La Niña probabilities for the same overlapping three-month season. Neutral odds refer to that whole target period. They are not the share of days that will be neutral or the chance of normal weather in a city.</p><p class="body-copy">A rising neutral probability is not an exact end date for an event. Compare the same target season across issues and retain the other two published probabilities. A forecast ending in summer cannot determine the following winter. See <a href="/forecast/when-will-it-end/">how the peak, official phase and regional impacts follow different clocks</a>.</p></section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Questions about neutral conditions</h2>{questions.map(item => <div class="lifecycle-answer"><h3>{item.question}</h3><p class="body-copy">{item.answer}</p></div>)}</section>
    <section class="lifecycle-section"><h2>Sources and next steps</h2><p class="body-copy">Definitions and circulation checked November 12, 2026: <a href="https://www.weather.gov/mhx/ensowhat">NWS: What is ENSO?</a> Forecast interpretation: <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/enso/roni/outlook/">CPC ENSO outlook</a> and <a href="https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/predictions/long_range/">CPC regional outlooks</a>. Compare <a href="/impacts/">regional impacts</a> and the <a href="/winter-outlook/">dated U.S. winter guide</a>. This page is an evergreen explanation, separate from the live phase-probability feed.</p></section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: questions.map(item => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: item.question, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.answer } })) }).replace(/</g, '\\u003c')} />
</Layout>
